+## Selected new stdin policy; no character-device equivalence
+
+Root's source-only selection is qa/p212_preprobe_native_mask_root01/RECEPTION.md.
+It selects a new policy, not satisfaction of the old character-device requirement.
+The only prospective stdin path is:
+
+`/root/autodl-tmp/symbolic_dynamics/docs/papers211_215_sequence/qa/p212_keyed_stdin_input01/empty.stdin`
+
+Its exact descriptor has role=stdin, kind=file, comparison=stable,
+resolved equal to that same path, symlink_target=null, members=null, physical
+ancestors, all fourteen actual lstat/stat fields, regular mode and size "0".
+Its complete content is {bytes:0,sha256:e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855}.
+The inner policy and prepared Node request use precisely
+{path:<that literal>,role:"stdin",kind:"file",content:<that whole pin>,handoff_offset:0}.
+No future descriptor, inode, mask or observed content is supplied by this source.
+
+The observer substitutes this required max_bytes=0/capture_hex=true regular
+target for the old /dev/null metadata target; /dev is no longer a needed
+component of this frontier. It requires an entirely physical chain and a
+regular zero-size native observation, then preserves its existing same-fd
+whole-file/overflow-failure discipline and checks the actual empty pin/hex.
+No native mask bit, expected-key-before-body check, planned pass or closing
+control was removed. Other unsupported objects still stop; this does not
+repair or waive the original /dev statx failure.
+
+Python's separately authorized probe replaces its mandatory /dev/null
+metadata target with this exact input. It checks full_stat native support,
+regular mode and zero size but does not claim that metadata-only output
+opened or handed off stdin. Node's probe changes only its self-location.
+
+Both driver and outer validate the exact stdin descriptor and all physical
+ancestors, open O_RDONLY|O_NOFOLLOW|O_NONBLOCK, inspect the actual same fd
+against the earlier complete expected key before any byte read, request one
+current-position byte, require return zero, and check the same fd again.
+An unexpected byte is retained as a failure sentinel before refusal.
+Their ordinary full-input reads route stdin through this helper too.
+Outer before/after records retain actual full_stat masks and raw buffers on
+successful decoding. Node's fourteen Stats fields alone never establish
+native 0xFFF support: a separate actually received native key is mandatory.
+
+Fresh open creates a private open-file description initially at offset zero.
+Only the zero-return current-position read precedes handoff, so offset zero
+is source-derived, explicitly labelled, and not an observed lseek/fdinfo
+result. No such extra query is added. Ordinary open/read semantics support
+that source reasoning; actual later launch and behavior still require
+independent receipt. O_NONBLOCK supplies no hard regular-file time bound.
+[open(2)](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/open.2.html),
+[read(2)](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/read.2.html),
+[Node v22.22.2 fs source documentation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nodejs/node/v22.22.2/doc/api/fs.md).
+
+The collector adds a literal Bash input redirection from this same path.
+Bash's early open is inside the explicit ordinary-trusted bootstrap boundary,
+not independently keyed fd attestation before Bash/env/Python startup.
+The outer freshly opens and validates its own later Node stdin fd; the driver
+does the same for each separately permitted native tool call. These are
+distinct open-file descriptions, not proof of one fd shared through every
